\tag{fdip}
\title{Gauge actions induced by matter}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{A [connection background](fdip-006D) determines a positive matter
precision. Integrating out the matter field makes its determinant a
function of that fixed connection. On a regular lattice, a convergent
expansion writes this function as a sum over closed walks. The shortest
walks enclosing a square produce a plaquette term, while walks winding
around short periodic directions explain why the lattice periods matter.}

\p{Real and complex fields have different determinant exponents.
Keeping those exponents and the reference volume explicit fixes both
the induced coupling and the derivative interpreted as a current.
The following statements are finite-volume identities and a controlled
large-mass expansion. A joint probability law for matter and a varying
connection requires an additional choice of gauge measure and action.}

